= Ironic process theory =

== Overview ==
'''Ironic process theory''' is Daniel Wegner's theory of mental control proposing that attempts to control thoughts or mental states can involve two interacting processes:

* an effortful operating process that searches for content consistent with the desired mental state;
* a monitoring process that searches for signs that control is failing.

Under some conditions, especially limited cognitive capacity, monitoring for failure can increase accessibility of the very content being controlled.

== Operating process ==
The operating process deliberately seeks distractors, alternative thoughts, or states that satisfy the control goal.

It consumes cognitive resources.

== Monitoring process ==
The monitoring process checks for evidence that the unwanted state is returning.

To detect failure, the system must remain sensitive to representations related to that unwanted state.

This creates the possibility of an ironic effect.

== Cognitive load prediction ==
When cognitive resources are available, the operating process can counteract monitored unwanted content.

Under high [[Cognitive load]], the operating process may weaken while monitoring continues, increasing accessibility of the target.

This is one of the theory's most distinctive predictions.

== Thought suppression ==
[[Thought suppression]] is one domain in which ironic processes were studied.

The theory has also been applied to emotional control, performance, sleep, movement, and self-regulation.

These applications vary in evidentiary support.

== Evidence and limits ==
Classic studies support some ironic and rebound effects.

However, newer reviews challenge strong claims that thought suppression is generally ineffective or inevitably counterproductive.

Ironic process theory should therefore be treated as an influential explanatory model with conditional evidence, not a universal law of mental control.

== References ==
* Daniel M. Wegner, “Ironic Processes of Mental Control”, ''Psychological Review'' 101 (1994).
* Daniel M. Wegner, classic experimental literature on ironic mental control and cognitive load.
* Aneta Niczyporuk, “The Effectiveness and Adaptiveness of Suppressing Unwanted Thoughts”, ''Psychonomic Bulletin & Review'' 33 (2026), article first published online 2025; DOI 10.3758/s13423-025-02763-w.

